WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:09.460
Puerto Rico, the island of Ricky Martin, Bad
Bunny, Daddy Yankee and piña coladas is also

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.800
known for its unusual political status: because
you see, Visual Politik viewers, this island

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.660
is not a state, but part an unincorporated
territory of the United States of America.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.279
However, this could change very soon: Will
Puerto Rico become the 51st state of the United

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:24.279
States?

00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:25.820
In this video we tell you all the details.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:31.220
Its status is as unusual as it is controversial,
because Puerto Rico, despite being under US

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.880
sovereignty, does not participate in the vote
for president, nor does it vote for senators

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.020
or congressmen with voting rights.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:40.800
Despite this, Puerto Ricans have time and
again ratified their desire to remain within

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:41.800
this scheme.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.160
However, in recent years something has been
changing.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.550
The question arises: Are Puerto Ricans ready
to take the next step?

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.150
Where is the island of enchantment headed?

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.780
Well, in this video we are going to tell you
all the details.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:01:06.890
Let's get started.

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.930
Puerto Rico has become the largest economy
in the Caribbean and the standard of living

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.080
on this island is reasonably high.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.460
Obviously being closely connected to the North
American giant has certain benefits.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.110
And perhaps that is why both the government
and the islanders themselves have practically

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.390
never embarked on any independence venture.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.330
They have always been very attached to their
Commonwealth regime.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:28.590
However, the last few years have not been
easy and the model seems increasingly out

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.909
of date and is losing support: in the end,
Puerto Ricans are US citizens, yes, but they

00:01:33.909 --> 00:01:38.189
have no real representation in Congress, they
do not vote for federal authorities and their

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:40.890
justice system is not entirely autonomous.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.430
So in a way Puerto Rico is still a colony.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:47.460
Perhaps that is why Puerto Ricans themselves
have said ENOUGH is enough, it is time to

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.460
move forward...

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.149
Not to move away from the United States, but
rather to fully integrate with the North American

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:53.149
giant.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:57.210
And the question is, is this really possible,
and will we soon see Puerto Rico become the

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.070
51st state of the union?

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.020
What could such a change mean for the island?

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.210
Today, in this video we are going to answer
these questions, but before we do that, let’s

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.250
take a look at a little history to understand
where it all began.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.610
Let's get into it.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:16.989
FROM MADRID TO FORAKER AND JONES)

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:22.569
During his second voyage to the American continent,
Christopher Columbus came across the island

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:24.569
we all know today as Puerto Rico.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:30.250
Its capital, San Juan, was so named, precisely,
in honor of Prince John, then Prince of Asturias

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:34.220
and heir to the throne of Castile and Aragon
in what is now Spain.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.300
From that moment on and for more than 400
years, the Spanish crown maintained control

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.540
of the island until eventually a confrontation
between Spain and the United States changed

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:47.680
this relationship forever in the 19th century.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.709
In 1895 the Cuban War broke out.

00:02:52.709 --> 00:02:57.099
In this conflict, pro-independence revolutionary
rebels confronted the Spanish royalists.

00:02:57.099 --> 00:03:00.959
The pressure for the Crown was increasing
and its position was more and more compromised.

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:05.819
To try to alleviate the situation and the
increasingly strong pro-independence sentiment,

00:03:05.819 --> 00:03:10.360
Madrid recognized both Cuba and Puerto Rico
as autonomous regions in 1897.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.740
However, it was not enough and, what’s more,
Uncle Sam's help ended up backing the rebels.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:20.940
Finally, in 1898, a political-military clash
between Spain and the United States ended

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.319
with the resounding defeat of the old empire.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:28.190
The peace agreement that was reached established
that Cuba would remain under American guardianship

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:32.790
while Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines
became direct colonial dependencies of the

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.599
United States.

00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:40.489
For the first time the United States demonstrated
not only its strength, but above all its status

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:47.739
as a power and its ability to intervene abroad
to limit the influence of other foreign powers.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:53.629
However, despite this, it was not entirely
clear what its relationship would be with

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:56.610
these new territories that it now had under
his control.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.159
Let's just say that in Washington they didn't
want to emulate the old colonial scheme of

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:01.790
the Spanish or the British Empires.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:02.870
[Which makes sense.

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.920
After all this country emerged as a reaction
against the very concept of colony].

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:12.790
Well, it was in 1900, when the then senator
from Ohio, Joseph B. Foraker, led the drafting

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.570
and approval of a law that bore his own surname,
the Foraker Act; a law that established the

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:21.310
new political scheme that would be applied
from that moment on in Puerto Rico.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:25.070
For instance, this law determined that the
President of the United States himself would

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:29.759
appoint a governor for four years, who would
govern alongside an Executive Council in which

00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:35.780
at least 5 of its 11 members would have to
be Puerto Ricans.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:43.720
Simultaneously, a small local House of Delegates,
consisting of 35 members chosen by the local

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.810
population, began to function on the island.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.620
For its part, the United States District Court
for Puerto Rico was created to manage the

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.430
island's judicial system, and an appeals process
to the US Supreme Court was established.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:58.940
Also, despite not being a state, Puerto Ricans
would be able to elect a "Resident Commissioner,"

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:04.990
who would have a voice but no vote in the
US House of Representatives.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:08.990
The stakes would be raised just 17 years later.

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:17.440
In 1917, the Jones-Shafroth Act, signed by
then-President Woodrow Wilson, struck a blow

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.039
with its Article 5.

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:20.090
Just take a look at it.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.990
("All citizens of Puerto Rico [...] and all
natives of Puerto Rico who were temporarily

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:29.400
absent from the Island on 11 April 1899, and
have thereafter returned to and are permanently

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.590
residing in said island, and are not citizens
of any foreign country, are hereby declared

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:37.750
to be citizens of the United States, and shall
be considered and held as such.”

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:39.389
Jones-Shafroth Act (1917))

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:43.900
Suddenly, something very unusual for the time
was done: Puerto Ricans were recognized as

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.600
citizens of the United States.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.120
An unprecedented privilege: Puerto Ricans
were not born in a formal US state, but nevertheless

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.259
enjoyed the same rights.

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:53.600
[By the way, have you noticed what year this
law was passed?

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:54.600
Well...

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.680
I say this because it is said that this initiative
had a lot to do with increasing the recruiting

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.539
base in the midst of World War One.

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:03.400
Of course, we can't know for sure if that
was the deciding factor.]

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:13.039
However, the Jones-Shafroth Act also created
a locally elected and much more representative

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:17.650
bicameral legislature, a chamber that became
competent to legislate on island affairs.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:21.570
Of course, both the governor of the island
and even, if necessary, the President of the

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.530
United States had the right of veto.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:28.120
The point is that, in only 17 years, Puerto
Ricans became US citizens and set in motion

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.840
the prototype of their civil government.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.220
But what exactly is this island?

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.220
A colony?

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.150
A territory subordinate to the United States?

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:38.460
While it’s true that Puerto Ricans are US
citizens, the relationship of their land to

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:40.760
the rest of the country is quite different.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:41.760
Listen up.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.690
By the way, before we continue, if you are
someone who likes the world of economics,

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.120
don't forget to take a look at our new sister
channel: VisualEconomik.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.490
We leave you the link in the description and
up here

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.000
COMMONW...

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.010
AH NO, UNINCORPORATED TERRITORY)

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:05.750
The government of Puerto Rico continued to
be elected from the Oval Office until 1947,

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:10.530
when Act 447 of the US Congress established
the possibility that the island's Governor

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.040
would finally be elected by the people themselves.

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.780
Thus Luis Muñoz Marín became the first governor
elected by Puerto Ricans.

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:22.330
His administration lasted no less than 16
years, and was absolutely key to the island's

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:23.330
status.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:27.190
For example, in 1950, Muñoz Marín managed
to reach an agreement with Washington for

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:32.320
the approval of Act 600, which gave Puerto
Rico the possibility of having its own "state

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.160
constitution", as the rest of the states of
the Union already had.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.340
It was a kind of fundamental law that established
the organization of the territory.

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:44.090
Well, in this text, which finally came into
force in 1952, it establishes that Puerto

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:48.650
Rico is itself a "Commonwealth" attached to
the United States, but since the text is also

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:53.000
written in Spanish, the translation speaks
of a "Freely Associated State", which is the

00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:00.370
status that the island still holds officially
today...

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:01.720
This marked a turning point.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:06.150
From that moment on, Muñoz Marín's own idea
was that Puerto Rico should have more and

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.150
more autonomy.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:10.570
As you are about to see, this is essentially
the idea that modern Puerto Rico is founded

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:14.630
on and it defines its particular relationship
with the United States.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:15.630
Take a look.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:22.530
There will always be some areas of authority
that we will delegate which will be defense,

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:29.820
legislation inherent in common citizenship,
legislation inherent in common currency and

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:33.320
a few other things like that.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:43.110
But the progress is that every day, as the
commonwealth grows, the votes that you and

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:51.980
all Puerto Ricans cast at the ballot box will
have greater authority over our lives.")

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:56.130
However, not everything was so harmonious.

00:08:56.130 --> 00:09:04.829
In 1950 the Jayuya Uprising took place, a
revolt led by armed pro-independence sectors

00:09:04.829 --> 00:09:07.000
that was quickly put down by the security
forces.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.860
Let's just say that the whole free associated
State thing was not really apparent.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.630
Be that as it may, in 1952, President Dwight
Eisenhower gave the go-ahead but demanded

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:19.590
that a referendum be held to ascertain the
true will of Puerto Ricans.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:22.399
The elections were held on 4 November of that
same year.

00:09:22.399 --> 00:09:23.910
And, do you want to know the result?

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:24.910
Well...

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:29.940
No less than 87.8% of the islanders voted
in favor of the new constitutional framework

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.590
establishing the commonwealth.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.020
The support was, as you can see, overwhelming,
an absolute majority.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.490
This would be one of the many referendums
in which Puerto Ricans have expressed their

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.670
support for maintaining ties with Washington:

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:45.029
We could almost say that the second half of
the 20th century was a kind of honeymoon period

00:09:45.029 --> 00:09:47.920
between the continental United States and
Puerto Rico.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:53.790
Look at this.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:57.450
SANCHEZ VALLE, THE ARMS DEALER WHO CHANGED
HISTORY)

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:02.840
The second half of the 20th century was a
bed of roses for the Puerto Rican economy.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:07.880
Around 1950, the US federal government introduced
a package of tax incentives for U.S. companies

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:11.410
to settle on the island, generating a huge
economic boom.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:15.980
Look at the difference with the rest of Latin
America.Construction, hotels, businesses,

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:17.490
new highways...

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.060
Puerto Rico's economy began to flourish.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:23.430
And of course this had significant social
implications: poverty was reduced and practically

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:24.709
all parameters improved.

00:10:24.709 --> 00:10:27.650
For years Puerto Rico was considered a success
story.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:31.940
And take note, because since the first referendum
was held in 1952, Puerto Ricans have been

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.910
called to the polls six times to express their
opinion on the sovereignty status of the island.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:43.310
They did so in 1967, 1993, 1998, 2012, 2017
and 2020.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:46.839
Well, in all these public surveys, the link
with Washington won...

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:51.079
But in different ways.

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:55.810
While the commonwealth concept won until 2012,
as of that year, the option in favor of becoming

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.560
another state, a formal state of the United
States, gained a clear majority.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.149
And the question is, what caused such a leap?

00:11:02.149 --> 00:11:05.230
Why did Puerto Ricans cease to be satisfied
with their current status?

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:09.920
Well, in order to answer that question we
have to go back to September 2008, when Luis

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.650
Sanchez del Valle was captured on the island
for illegal arms trafficking.

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:18.040
He was simultaneously indicted on that charge
by both the US and Puerto Rican courts.

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:21.930
The defendant then sued the State of the island
by declaring that a person could not be charged

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:27.040
twice for the same crime in the same jurisdiction,
thus framing the renowned case Puerto Rico

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:31.940
v. Sanchez Valle, which was resolved in 2015
with a controversial ruling:

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:36.281
C1("Puerto Rico's authority to prosecute persons
derives from delegation by the United States

00:11:36.281 --> 00:11:40.690
Congress and not by virtue of any sovereignty
of its own (...) The adoption of a constitution,

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:44.411
by delegation of Congress, to organize local
government in substitution for much of the

00:11:44.411 --> 00:11:48.800
organic law then in force, did not represent
a cession of sovereignty to Puerto Rico.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.470
To the contrary, Puerto Rico did not cease
to be a territory of the United States."

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:53.779
Puerto Rico Supreme Court Doctrine)

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:57.170
And you may be wondering, so what is so controversial
about this?

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:01.490
Basically, the courts made it clear that the
sovereignty of Puerto Rico does not rest with

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:04.529
Puerto Ricans, but rather remains in the hands
of Washington.

00:12:04.529 --> 00:12:08.060
Over the years, this concept, along with economic
problems that we have already told you about

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:15.010
in past videos, suddenly triggered the ambition
to change Puerto Rico's status.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:22.800
In 2017, for example, the island was practically
bankrupt with an external debt in excess of

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:23.800
$70 billion.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:28.900
In fact it was not until January 2022 that
a federally appointed financial oversight

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:31.850
committee managed to restructure the public
debt with creditors.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:35.209
But, of course, there is still a long way
to go before the problem can be considered

00:12:35.209 --> 00:12:36.209
solved.

00:12:36.209 --> 00:12:37.810
But, do you know what is most curious of all?

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:42.240
The funny thing is that many of Puerto Rico's
economic problems, if not the vast majority,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:45.480
have nothing to do with being a Commonwealth
or the 51st state.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.080
We're talking about things like the Jones
Merchant Marine Act passed in 1920 and still

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.089
in effect today.

00:12:51.089 --> 00:12:54.860
This is a law that prohibits foreign-flagged
ships from transporting goods between two

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:55.860
US ports.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:59.890
This makes moving things to and from Puerto
Rico very expensive... which obviously erodes

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:02.070
a lot of the island's competitiveness.

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.070
Why is that?

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:07.029
Well because US flagged and crewed ships are
much, much more expensive to operate.

00:13:07.029 --> 00:13:11.300
As an example, this law makes it impossible
for large international freighters to unload

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:15.380
goods destined for Puerto Rico on their way
to or from the US.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:19.970
Instead those goods have to make a much more
convoluted journey to get to the island.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:24.910
Imagine, Shanghai-Texas, reload goods onto
a US-flagged ship, Texas-Puerto Rico.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:28.700
And then we also need to talk about things
like the minimum wage and federal subsidy

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:29.700
plans.

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.010
But in order to understand this, look at this
chart.

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:38.110
As you can see, Puerto Rico is much, much
poorer than the rest of the United States.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:42.590
However, since it has to apply the same federal
minimum wage or an abnormally high level of

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:46.310
social benefits in relation to what the parameters
of the island actually are.

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:48.240
And here the problem is evident.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:51.730
What is acceptable for Texas does not have
to be the same for Puerto Rico.

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.920
You could say that federal policies and regulations
mean that when it comes down to it, very few

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.630
people work in Puerto Rico.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:58.839
That is one of the main problems.

00:13:58.839 --> 00:14:02.589
Although, yes it’s true that things are
improving thanks to new tax incentives for

00:14:02.589 --> 00:14:04.560
companies setting up on the island.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:08.360
Well, when it comes down to it, the fact is
that none of these problems would change if

00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.440
Puerto Rico ceases to be a commonwealth and
becomes just another US state.

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:16.670
In fact, some of its tax benefits for businesses
and investors could disappear altogether.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:21.149
Yes, the island would gain more integration,
influence and probably government investment...

00:14:21.149 --> 00:14:23.060
But it would leave many other things by the
wayside.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:26.790
Be that as it may, the option of becoming
another state is the number one favorite of

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:28.029
Puerto Ricans.

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:31.579
Take a look.

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:37.900
THE STATUS THAT WENT OUT OF FASHION)

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:45.180
In 2020, Puerto Rico held its latest referendum,
but this time neither commonwealth nor independence

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.180
was an option.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:50.600
The question was much clearer: "Should Puerto
Rico be admitted immediately into the Union

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:51.600
as a state?"

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.870
This is exactly the question that was asked
in that referendum.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:57.930
Well, the yes vote obtained more than 50%
of the votes, which gave the island's government

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.699
the strength to lobby in Washington.

00:14:59.699 --> 00:15:04.529
It was a campaign in which the current governor,
Pedro Pierluisi, is involved, but which requires

00:15:04.529 --> 00:15:07.760
overcoming a very complicated legislative
circuit.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.370
For example, the House of Representatives
must pass a law that enables a binding referendum

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:15.490
for the federal government, that is, an election
that not only allows citizens to express their

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:20.440
opinion – as has been the case up to now
– but also has legal and political consequences

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.820
that allow the President of the United States
to sign the incorporation of the new state.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:31.440
Well, in December 2022, with 233 votes in
favor and 191 against, the Lower House surprisingly

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.490
succeeded in passing House Bill 8393.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.430
The first step was thus completed, but there
is still the Senate...

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.959
And that, VisualPolitik community, is not
exactly a piece of cake.

00:15:40.959 --> 00:15:41.959
Why?

00:15:41.959 --> 00:15:46.259
Well, basically because Puerto Rico is a very
democratic territory politically.

00:15:46.259 --> 00:15:54.500
In other words, many Republicans are afraid
that what already happened with Hawaii will

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:59.130
happen again, that Puerto Rico will be a Democratic
stronghold that will swing the majority in

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:03.630
the chambers, particularly, in the non-proportional
Senate, where all states, regardless of their

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:06.290
size or population, have two representatives.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:10.639
C2("We have to go senator by senator explaining
to them why it is fair to give equal rights

00:16:10.639 --> 00:16:12.920
to American citizens residing in Puerto Rico.

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:16.570
We also have to explain to them that Puerto
Rico is diverse, that neither should Republican

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:19.940
senators think that if Puerto Rico becomes
a state automatically that means that there

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:22.540
will be 2 senators and 4 Democratic congressmen."

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:25.089
Pedro Pierluisi, Governor of Puerto Rico)

00:16:25.089 --> 00:16:26.209
So the whole thing seems complicated.

00:16:26.209 --> 00:16:27.209
But who knows.

00:16:27.209 --> 00:16:32.000
In any case, the Puerto Rican government has
already set 5 November 2023 as the date for

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.130
a new referendum, hoping that by then the
Senate will have given the green light and

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:40.769
these elections can be the final step to becoming
the 51st star on the American flag.

00:16:40.769 --> 00:16:44.610
Or at least the 51st state, because it seems
that the flag will not be changed.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:48.709
We will see what impact there will be among
Puerto Ricans if the legislature closes the

00:16:48.709 --> 00:16:49.709
doors again.

00:16:49.709 --> 00:16:50.740
But for now it's your turn:

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:52.959
Do you think Puerto Rico should become a new
state?

00:16:52.959 --> 00:16:54.449
What benefits would this option have?

00:16:54.449 --> 00:16:55.930
What do you think about the commonwealth status?

00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:59.149
Leave us your comments here and if you found
this video interesting don't forget to like

00:16:59.149 --> 00:17:00.620
and subscribe to VisualPolitik.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:01.899
Thank you very much for being there.

00:17:01.899 --> 00:17:03.080
All the best and see you next time.

